  "worked_solution": "We model the number of particles in the substance using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-3N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $8\\,500$ particles initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=8\\,500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of particles $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of particles when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =8\\,500\\,{e}^{-6} \\\\  & \u224821.069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $21$ particles remain.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
